course: dictionary
lesson: 14353
title: The word omelette
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An omelette is a noun, so it names a thing you can eat. Its plural is omelettes. Some people spell it omelet, with one t at the end, and both spellings are right.
explain: Look at the clues in a sentence. «Mum beat three eggs and cooked them flat in the pan to make an omelette.» Beaten eggs and a frying pan tell you what kind of food it is.
explain: The word also appears in a saying: «You can't make an omelette without breaking eggs.» Here it means you cannot do something big without some problems along the way.
fact: An omelette is made from beaten eggs cooked flat in a frying pan.
fact: Omelettes are often folded over a filling such as cheese, ham or vegetables.
fact: Omelette can also be spelled omelet.
fact: Hot pans can burn you, so handle them carefully.
fact: Wash your hands after touching raw eggs.
exercise: «Ravi beat three eggs and cooked them flat in a pan to make an omelette.» What is an omelette? | A pan used to crack eggs | *A dish of beaten eggs cooked flat in a frying pan | A bowl for mixing cake | A kind of bread roll
exercise: «Ella folded her omelette over some cheese and ham.» What was inside the omelette? | Soap and water | Sugar and jam | *Cheese and ham | Nothing at all, it was empty
exercise: What does the saying «You can't make an omelette without breaking eggs» mean? | Cooking is always easy | *You cannot do something big without some problems | Eggs must always be broken with a spoon | Only chefs can make omelettes
test: Which word is another spelling of omelette? | *Omelet | Omellet | Omlet | Omelete
test: What should you do after touching raw eggs? | *Wash your hands | Put them in your pocket | Dry them on your hair | Lick your fingers
exam: Mia put cheese and mushrooms in her pan and cooked them gently. What did she make? | A chocolate cake | Mushrooms only | *An omelette with a filling | Cheese only
